% --- RECALL (1) ---
\textbf{Recall:}  
Provide the formal definition of a **defect** in software engineering as given in the section.

% --- UNDERSTAND (1) ---
\textbf{Understand 1:}  
Explain what is meant by **defect injection**.  Identify one development phase (e.g., requirements analysis, design, or coding) where defect injection occurs and give a brief example of a defect that could be injected in that phase.

% --- UNDERSTAND (2) ---
\textbf{Understand 2:}  
Explain what is meant by **defect removal**.  Identify one quality‑control activity (e.g., review, unit testing, integration testing, or acceptance testing) that performs defect removal and illustrate with a short example of a defect that could be found and eliminated by that activity.

% --- APPLY (1) ---
\textbf{Apply 1:}  
A project has discovered 45 defects in a product that is 90 KLOC in size.  
Calculate the **defect density** (defects per KLOC). Show your work.

% --- APPLY (2) ---
\textbf{Apply 2:}  
The quality plan for a 30 KLOC library‑management system targets a defect density of 0.5 defects/KLOC.  
Using the target, determine the maximum number of defects allowed in the product. Then state whether the defect density you computed in the previous question (45 defects / 90 KLOC) meets, exceeds, or is below this target, and briefly explain what this indicates about the project’s quality.

% --- ANALYSE (1) ---
\textbf{Analyse:}  
Two quality plans are proposed for a new application:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Plan A}: Relies heavily on automated testing (unit, integration, system) and allocates only 10 % of the total defect‑removal effort to manual code reviews.
  \item \textbf{Plan B}: Emphasizes manual code reviews (40 % of defect‑removal effort) and uses automated testing for the remaining verification.
\end{itemize}

Compare these plans in terms of expected defect‑removal effectiveness. Discuss the strengths and weaknesses of each approach, considering the types of defects each activity is best at finding.

% --- EVALUATE (1) ---
\textbf{Evaluate:}  
A quality plan sets a target defect density of 0.1 defects/KLOC for a 50 KLOC system, which would allow only 5 defects in total.  
 argue whether this target is realistic given typical defect‑injection rates and the removal percentages shown in the worked example. Propose a more attainable target (state the new defect density and the corresponding allowed defect count) and justify why your revised target better balances quality goals with practical project constraints.
